**Extortion (Section 383 IPC)** involves intentionally putting a person in fear of injury to dishonestly induce them to deliver property.

**Robbery (Section 390 IPC)** is an aggravated form of theft or extortion involving the use of force, fear of instant death/hurt/wrongful restraint, or an attempt to cause such fear at the time of committing the offence.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Criminal breach of trust (Section 405 IPC)** involves property that was lawfully entrusted to the accused, who subsequently misappropriates it, so the dishonest intent arises after lawful possession.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Criminal conspiracy (Section 120A IPC)** is a distinct substantive offence constituted merely by an **agreement** between two or more persons to commit an illegal act, even if the act is never carried out, whereas abetment by conspiracy under Section 107 additionally requires an act or illegal omission in pursuance of that conspiracy.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Abduction (Section 362 IPC)** applies to persons of any age and involves compelling or inducing a person by force, compulsion, or deceitful means to go from any place, but it is **not a substantive offence by itself** — it becomes punishable only when linked with a specific intention, such as under Sections 364-366 IPC.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Wrongful confinement (Section 340 IPC)** is a more serious form, involving the wrongful restraint of a person within certain circumscribed limits beyond which they cannot move, punishable under **Section 342 IPC** with a higher sentence.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

In a **non-cognizable offence**, the police cannot arrest without a warrant and must obtain the magistrate''s order to investigate; such offences are generally less serious in nature.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **non-bailable offence** is one where bail is **not a matter of right** but lies in the discretion of the court, which must consider factors like the gravity of the offence, likelihood of the accused fleeing or tampering with evidence.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Section 149 IPC** applies specifically to members of an **unlawful assembly** (minimum five persons with a common object) and fixes constructive liability even on members who did not actively participate, so long as the offence was committed in prosecution of, or was likely in pursuit of, the assembly''s common object.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Section 304 Part I** applies where the act was done with the **intention** of causing death or such bodily injury as is likely to cause death (but falls within an exception to Section 300), punishable with life imprisonment or up to 10 years plus fine.

**Section 304 Part II** applies where the act was done with mere **knowledge** that it was likely to cause death, without intention to cause death or such injury, punishable with up to 10 years, or fine, or both.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Section 351 IPC** defines **assault** as a gesture or preparation intending or knowing it is likely to cause apprehension that criminal force is about to be used, punishable under Section 352 IPC with imprisonment up to **3 months**, or fine, or both.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

An **invitation to offer** (e.g., display of goods in a shop window, advertisements, tenders, or auction notices) is merely an invitation for others to make offers, which the inviter may accept or reject, as established in *Pharmaceutical Society of Great Britain v. Boots Cash Chemists* (1953).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Indian law uniquely permits consideration to move from a **third person** (unlike English law), a principle illustrated in *Chinnaya v. Ramayya* (1882).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **voidable contract** (Section 2(i)) is one enforceable at the option of one party (usually the aggrieved party whose consent was not free) but not at the option of the other; it remains valid until the aggrieved party chooses to rescind it.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Misrepresentation (Section 18)** involves an innocent false statement made **without any intent to deceive**, believing it to be true. Both render the contract voidable, but fraud may additionally give rise to a claim in the tort of deceit.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A unilateral mistake of fact by one party (Section 22) does not generally render a contract voidable, while **mistake of law** in force in India (Section 21) does not affect the validity of a contract, though mistake of foreign law is treated as mistake of fact.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **wagering agreement (Section 30)** involves parties having no interest in the outcome other than the sum won or lost, and is expressly **void**, unlike a contingent contract which can be perfectly valid and enforceable.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A breach of contract, by contrast, arises from the violation of a duty fixed by the **parties themselves** through mutual agreement, and damages may be liquidated (pre-agreed) or unliquidated.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Public nuisance** affects the public at large or a class of the community and is generally a criminal offence (actionable civilly only on proof of special/particular damage by an individual), whereas **private nuisance** affects a specific individual''s enjoyment of their own property and is actionable as a civil wrong without need for special damage.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Special damages** are quantifiable pecuniary losses that must be specifically pleaded and proved (e.g., medical bills).

**Nominal damages** are a token sum awarded where a legal right is infringed but no substantial loss occurred.

**Exemplary/punitive damages** are awarded, over and above compensation, to punish the defendant for particularly oppressive, arbitrary, or unconstitutional conduct.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **contract of guarantee (Section 126)** involves a promise to perform the promise, or discharge the liability, of a third person in case of their default — it involves **three parties**: the principal debtor, the creditor, and the surety.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **warranty** is a stipulation **collateral** to the main purpose of the contract, the breach of which gives rise only to a claim for **damages**, without a right to reject the goods or treat the contract as at an end — as defined under Sections 12(2) and 12(3) of the Sale of Goods Act, 1930.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **contract** (Section 2(h)) is an agreement that is **enforceable by law**. Thus every contract is an agreement, but every agreement is not necessarily a contract — an agreement becomes a contract only when it fulfils the conditions laid down in Section 10.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Several/independent tortfeasors** act independently of each other but happen to cause the same damage to the plaintiff (e.g., two separate negligent drivers causing a single collision), and each may be liable to the extent of their own contribution, though the plaintiff may still be able to recover the full amount from any one of them, who may then seek contribution from the others.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Its main types are: **temporary/interim injunction** (granted during the pendency of a suit under Order XXXIX CPC), **permanent/perpetual injunction** (granted finally upon the merits of the suit), **prohibitory injunction** (restraining a wrongful act), and **mandatory injunction** (compelling performance of a positive act to undo a wrongful act already done).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Procedural law** (also called adjective law) lays down the **mechanism and process** for enforcing substantive rights and remedies in courts (e.g., the Code of Civil Procedure, Code of Criminal Procedure), governing how a legal right is to be enforced or a wrong redressed.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Private law** governs relationships **between individuals** inter se, including contract law, tort law, property law, and family law.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **right in personam** is available only against a **specific person or persons** (e.g., a contractual right against the other contracting party), and cannot be enforced against the world at large.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Ownership** is the **ultimate legal right** to a thing, including the right to possess, use, and dispose of it, which may exist independently of actual physical possession (e.g., an owner who has leased out their property).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **criminal wrong** is considered a wrong against **society/the State** as a whole, prosecuted by the State, and punished through **imprisonment, fine, or other penal sanctions**, aimed at deterrence and retribution rather than mere compensation.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

An **original precedent** creates and applies a **new rule of law** where none previously existed, thereby contributing an entirely new principle to the body of case law — a significant source of judge-made law in common law systems.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Uncodified/common law** develops through **judicial precedent** and customary practice over time, without being consolidated into a single enacted code, as is characteristic of many areas of English law.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **directory provision** is one where substantial compliance suffices, and non-compliance does not automatically invalidate the action, though it may attract other consequences — courts determine the nature of a provision based on its **language, purpose, and the consequences of non-compliance** intended by the legislature.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Res communis** refers to property that belongs to **everyone in common** and cannot be exclusively appropriated by any individual (e.g., the high seas, air), reflecting different classifications of ownership in classical property jurisprudence.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Judicial restraint** refers to courts deferring to the legislature and executive, confining themselves strictly to interpreting and applying existing law without venturing into policy-making, respecting the separation of powers.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Consent theories** (e.g., Locke, Rousseau) ground legitimate obligation in the (actual or hypothetical) **consent of the governed**, emphasising that authority derives its legitimacy from the people subject to it, not merely from coercive capacity.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Restorative justice** focuses on **repairing the harm** caused by the offence through reconciliation, dialogue between victim and offender, and reintegration of the offender into the community, rather than purely punitive measures.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Salmond** described ownership as the relationship between a person and a right vested in them, characterised by features such as the right to **possess, use, consume, destroy, or alienate** the thing, subject to the limits imposed by law.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

